artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a satellite communication system in which a legitimate satellite transmitter S serves a population of legitimate ground users U over a wireless channel, providing services such as broadband connectivity or Internet-of-Things (IoT) data exchange (i.e., services other than global navigation satellite systems). An adversary A is capable of (i) transmitting radio signals toward the legitimate receivers with power potentially exceeding that of S, and (ii) injecting fabricated messages without possessing the credentials of any user in U. Two attack models are considered:

1. Impersonation: A transmits signals crafted so that a receiver in U or at S accepts A as a legitimate party and grants it service access.
2. High-power spoofing: A transmits a higher-power copy of a forged signal that overrides the legitimate signal at the receiver, causing the receiver to decode A's message as authentic and thereby leaking information meant for legitimate users or granting illicit access.

Authentication is required at the physical layer, meaning the legitimacy of a transmitter must be verified using only physical-layer observables such as channel state information, hardware-induced radio-frequency fingerprints, Doppler signatures, angle-of-arrival, propagation delay, or other measurable channel/device features, without relying solely on higher-layer cryptographic protocols.

Problem. Design a physical-layer authentication and anti-spoofing scheme for such non-GNSS satellite services (broadband and satellite-IoT) and prove, under an explicit channel model (e.g., LEO/GEO fading with Doppler and large path loss) and an explicit adversary model (with bounded transmit power, location uncertainty, and hardware capabilities), formal guarantees of the following form:

(a) A false-acceptance probability (probability that A's signal is authenticated as legitimate) bounded by a prescribed ε > 0;
(b) A false-rejection probability (probability that a legitimate user is rejected) bounded by a prescribed δ > 0;
(c) Robustness against the high-power overshadowing attack, i.e., the guarantee in (a) holds even when A's received power at the verifier exceeds that of S by an arbitrary factor up to some specified threshold;
(d) Feasibility under satellite-specific constraints: limited onboard computation, long propagation delays, high Doppler shifts, intermittent visibility, and large numbers of low-complexity IoT terminals.

Provide either an explicit construction achieving (a)–(d) with quantitative bounds, or prove an impossibility result showing that no such scheme can simultaneously satisfy (a)–(d) within the stated model.
